      },
      "finding": "The two camera canvases are DrawingAreas promoted to native X11 windows; ximagesink writes full BGR frames through appsrc/videoconvert/videoscale. The GTK preview has no camera Pixbuf/Cairo image blend, accumulated alpha surface or independent face-box drawing. Existing Pixbuf uses in this module belong to avatar/identity-crop UI, not these native camera canvases. Scaling applies to the whole already rendered image; this GTK layer has no camera mirror/rotation or independent bbox-coordinate transform."
    },
    {
      "id": "NATIVE_AND_GTK_SURFACE_OWNERSHIP",
      "status": "MINIMAL_HYPOTHESIS_CANDIDATE_NOT_PHYSICAL_ROOT_CAUSE_PROOF",
      "locations": {
        "tools/board_avatar_gtk.py.before": [
          535,
          1049
        ],
        "tools/board_avatar_gtk.py.candidate": [
          535,
          537,
          538,
          1052,
          1054,
          1055
        ]
      },
      "finding": "The exact current source creates both native camera DrawingAreas without app_paintable or double-buffer ownership settings. GTK repaint/backing-store behavior on the same native drawable is therefore a plausible source of flicker that can be isolated without changing camera pixels or recognition. The candidate declares each native surface app-paintable and disables GTK3 double buffering. No custom draw handler, Cairo clearing, new queue or frame-clock work is introduced. Actual GTK/X11 rendering effect is untested here."
    },
    {
      "id": "LATEST_FRAME_QUEUE_AND_CLEAR",
      "status": "SOURCE_CONFIRMED_GUARDS_RETAINED",
